On Dec 20, 10:24 pm, Prometheus77 <aarons...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I've lost a lot of good early characters to magic traps.

In the very early game, the blind-and-surrounded effect of
a magic trap is frequently fatal, yes, and there is often
relatively little you can do.  I have managed to get away
from them alive in some cases (usually by blind fleeing,
much as others have suggested; on one occasion I actually
managed to get Elbereth written successfully while blind,
despite the odds), but I've also died plenty.  It's not
unusual for the generated monsters to be faster than your
character, for example, or sometimes the trap's placement
is such that you can't get past them.

But this is not really different from anything else in the
very early game.  There are a thousand ways to die in
NetHack.  Granted, several hundred of those are completely
avoidable -- but several hundred more aren't.  That's the
nature of NetHack.  It doesn't really care if you are able
to survive or not.  The game doesn't hold back on generating
stuff you might not be able to handle, based on how buff
your character is and what you have in inventory.  It just
generates stuff, at random, and you either can handle it
or not.  If not, too bad.  That's NetHack.  C'est la vie.
(Or, perhaps more apropos, c'est la mort.)

I would like to see a variant that makes a more concerted
effort to scale the difficulty up as the game progresses, so
that as the game proceeds it becomes *harder* to survive, as
in most games, rather than easier and easier, as in NetHack.
But such a variant would undeniably be VERY different from
vanilla NetHack.


